      "heading": "The Cabin Filter",
      "html": "<p>The cabin filter sits in the air path, usually behind the glove box or under the cowl at the base of the windscreen, and it catches dust, pollen, leaves, and other debris before they reach the evaporator and the cabin. A clean filter is white or pale; a dirty one is grey or black with debris caught in its pleats.</p>\n<p>A blocked filter cuts airflow, makes the blower work harder, and can make the evaporator freeze up, because less warm cabin air passes over it to keep it above freezing. It can also let musty smells through as trapped debris gets damp. Replacing it is a simple job on most cars — slide the old one out, note the airflow arrow, and slide the new one in the same way.</p>\n"
